---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="km-to-miles">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="km-to-miles" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Divide by 1.609344</h2>
    <p>
      Km to miles is a division by the exact figure 1.609344, or a multiplication by 0.621371192. Both
      give the same answer. The division is easier to remember and harder to mistype. 100 km is 62.14
      miles, 10 km is 6.21 miles, and 1 km is 0.621 miles.
    </p>

    <h2>Three mental shortcuts, ranked</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Trick</th><th scope="col">How</th><th scope="col">Error</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Multiply by 0.6</td><td>100 km &rarr; 60 mi</td><td>3.4% low. Fine for a rough idea</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fibonacci</td><td>Adjacent Fibonacci numbers: 8 km &asymp; 5 mi, 13 km &asymp; 8 mi, 21 km &asymp; 13 mi</td><td>Under 0.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Halve and add a tenth of the half twice</td><td>100 &rarr; 50 &rarr; 55 &rarr; 60.5, then a nudge</td><td>About 2% low</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The Fibonacci trick works because the golden ratio, 1.618, is within 0.6% of 1.609344. It is the
      most accurate mental conversion in any unit system, and it is easy to remember.
    </p>

    <h2>Driving in a metric country</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>50 km/h = 31 mph, the standard European urban limit</li>
      <li>80 km/h = 50 mph</li>
      <li>100 km/h = 62 mph</li>
      <li>120 km/h = 75 mph, a common motorway limit in Spain, Italy and Ireland</li>
      <li>130 km/h = 81 mph, the French autoroute limit in dry weather</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Fuel economy converts differently. Litres per 100 km is an inverse measure, so you cannot scale it
      directly into miles per gallon. You must invert and convert, and the gallon differs between the US
      (3.785 L) and the UK (4.546 L).
    </p>

    <h2>Race distances the other way</h2>
    <p>
      A 5K is 3.11 miles, a 10K is 6.21, a half marathon is 13.11 and a full marathon is 26.22. If
      your watch uses miles and the course uses kilometres, the markers and your splits drift apart:
      at 20 km you are at 12.43 miles, not 12.5.
    </p>

    <h2>Elevation, and why it is usually feet</h2>
    <p>
      Even in fully metric countries, aviation altitude is in feet, and many cycling and running apps
      use feet for climb by default. A 1,000 m ascent is 3,280.8 ft. The extras panel on this page shows
      feet next to miles, because one screen often gives climb and distance in different systems.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
